# bridge.conf.example — every value the Slack bridge installer reads.
#
#   cp bridge.conf.example bridge.conf && chmod 600 bridge.conf
#   $EDITOR bridge.conf                                      # seven values, none secret
#   python3 scripts/pipeline_bridge_setup.py compose         # read what it builds
#
# Then docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, step by step. The installer reports EVERY bad
# value in one pass.
#
# FORMAT: `KEY=value`, one per line. `#` starts a comment only at the start of a
# line. Parsed, never sourced. A key set twice is an error naming both lines.
# NO KEY HERE EVER HOLDS A CREDENTIAL: a value shaped like one is refused, by
# key name only. The bridge app's token goes in at `token`'s hidden prompt.
#
# It runs beside an existing Stage E install: the same role account, its clone
# of this kit, and its env file, which already holds the owner's tracker key.

# --- Required --------------------------------------------------------------

# The dispatcher's role account. The bridge runs as it (owner decision,
# 2026-10-10): its own Slack app keeps it apart from the chat bot.
ROLE_ACCOUNT=_exampledispatch

# That account's clone of this kit, which the job runs from: the one the Stage E
# daemons exec from. The Stage E installer's `code` step is the only thing that
# moves it, and stops this job while it does. The installer refuses any other
# clone: nothing would ever move it.
ROLE_KIT_CLONE=~/.stage-e/kit

# The private channel(s) the bridge reads and answers in: the chat bot's
# channel. Comma-separated. Invite the bridge app to each (card CK-B1).
CHANNEL_IDS=C0123456789

# The tracker user id of the owner, whose key the bridge acts with.
OWNER_USER_ID=00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001

# The work teams whose tickets the bridge will ask about. Comma-separated.
TEAM_KEYS=PROD

# The LaunchDaemon label, reverse-DNS.
JOB_LABEL=com.example.bridge

# The bridge app's own bot user id: its member ID, copied from the app's profile
# in Slack (card CK-B1). The job refuses any token that answers to another bot.
BOT_USER_ID=U0123456789

# --- Optional, with their defaults -----------------------------------------

# Whether a verified yes acts. no: the bridge asks and records only. yes: a
# verified yes to `plan <ticket id>` moves that idea into Plan it, and a yes to
# `approve <epic id>` moves the epic to READY_STATE and hands its children to
# AGENT_USER_NAME, in dependency order. Both act as the owner.
# Start with no, watch it ask, then set yes and run `install --apply`.
#ACT=no
#PLAN_IT_STATE=Plan it

# The dispatcher's app user, by its display name in the tracker: who an
# approved epic's children are handed to. Empty refuses every `approve`.
#AGENT_USER_NAME=
# How many children of one approved epic may be started and unfinished at once.
#EPIC_WIP=1
# The to-do state an approved epic moves to.
#READY_STATE=Todo
# The done state an approved epic moves to once every piece of it is merged.
# The bridge then posts a wrap-up, and asks before it files the review notes
# left as one new ticket in the team.
#DONE_STATE=Done
# The state a child sits in while its pull request waits for your merge. It is
# the lane a ticket moves to when review is done, and setup-board names it
# Needs Approval. Spell it as the tracker shows it, comma-separated if your teams
# name it differently. The bridge says a child there is waiting for you, and never
# tells you to hand it over again.
#HANDOFF_STATES=Needs Approval

# Answering a stuck session (docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, Step 10). The notifier's
# STATE_DIR, exactly as notifier.conf sets it: the bridge reads the pings the
# notifier records there. And the notifier app's own bot user id (its member ID,
# from its profile in Slack): with it, a reply under a ping the notifier did not
# record is refused aloud instead of ignored, and `verify` needs the pings file.
#NOTIFIER_STATE_DIR=~/.stage-e/state
#NOTIFIER_BOT_USER_ID=

# Watching the notifier (docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, Step 11). The notifier's
# JOB_LABEL, exactly as notifier.conf sets it. Each pass then reads how often
# launchd runs the notifier, from its plist, and its heartbeat in
# NOTIFIER_STATE_DIR. When the notifier is quiet for three of its intervals plus
# two minutes, keeps failing, or can't be read, the bridge says so once in the
# first channel, and once more when it is back. Keep INTERVAL_SECONDS at most
# half the notifier's, or `verify` says BLOCKED. launchd keeps the interval the
# job was loaded with, so after changing INTERVAL_SECONDS, reload the job: card
# CK-B3, then card CK-B2. Empty: the watch is off. Leave it empty only on a
# machine with no notifier.
#NOTIFIER_JOB_LABEL=

# The only Slack user ids who may answer a question. Empty means any full
# member of the workspace: guests, bots and other workspaces are always refused.
#ALLOWED_SLACK_USERS=

# Working in your DM (docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, "Working in your DM"). The Slack
# user ids whose DM with the bridge app it also reads and answers in, with
# messages of their own, so each one notifies. Comma-separated. Set it only
# once the app has the new manifest (card CK-B1). Empty: no DM.
#DM_USER_IDS=

# The writer (docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, "The writer"; card CK-B5). yes: a model
# with no tools writes each message as it is sent, on your Claude subscription.
# What to type and what a yes does are never its words. no: plain words.
#WRITER=no
# The claude program's full path, as `command -v claude` prints it. Needed with
# WRITER=yes: the job runs under launchd, whose PATH is short.
#WRITER_BIN=
# The model that writes them.
#WRITER_MODEL=haiku
# The NAME its token goes under. `writer-token` puts the token there, from
# `claude setup-token`. A name of its own, never another key's.
#WRITER_TOKEN_ENV=CLAUDE_WRITER_OAUTH_TOKEN

# Asking the tech lead from your DM (docs/BRIDGE-OPERATOR.md, "Asking the tech
# lead from your DM"). yes: anything you write in your DM that is not a command
# or a yes or no goes to the dispatcher's chat bot, as a message in
# BACK_ROOM_CHANNEL_ID that mentions it, and its answer comes back to your DM.
# Each question is a tech-lead turn. Needs DM_USER_IDS. CHAT_BOT_USER_ID is the
# chat bot's member ID, from its profile in Slack. BACK_ROOM_CHANNEL_ID must be
# one of CHANNEL_IDS; empty, it is the first of them. The chat bot must be a
# member of it, and everyone there reads each question and its answer.
#ASK_TECH_LEAD=no
#CHAT_BOT_USER_ID=
#BACK_ROOM_CHANNEL_ID=
# A question that starts a new thread with the tech lead carries your DM's
# recent conversation under it, whatever its age, so the tech lead has the
# context even after a weekend. FORWARD_HISTORY_MESSAGES is how many of the
# newest messages, 0 to 100 (0: none). FORWARD_HISTORY_CHARS caps the block,
# 1 to 20000; its oldest lines go first. Used only with ASK_TECH_LEAD=yes.
#FORWARD_HISTORY_MESSAGES=30
#FORWARD_HISTORY_CHARS=8000

# The dispatcher's own env file, where the chat bot's token lives. With it set,
# `token` also refuses the chat bot's token. It must be a different file from
# ENV_FILE: the dispatcher hands this one to every session it starts.
#DISPATCHER_ENV_FILE=

#ENV_FILE=~/.stage-e/env
# The NAME the bridge app's token is stored under. Never SLACK_BOT_TOKEN, the
# chat bot's own name.
#SLACK_TOKEN_ENV=BRIDGE_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN
# The NAME of the owner's tracker key, which Stage E already stores.
#LINEAR_KEY_ENV=STAGE_E_LINEAR_API_KEY
#BRIDGE_CONFIG=~/.stage-e/bridge.json
#STATE_DIR=~/.stage-e/state
#INTERVAL_SECONDS=60
#APP_NAME=Pipeline bridge
